<header><p class="st-kicker">prince bishopric · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Imperial Abbey of Kaisheim</h1><p class="st-meta">1656 CE – 1802 CE (approx.) · capital Kaisheim</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Wealthy Cistercian abbey near Donauwörth whose abbots won immediacy within the Empire and ruled villages across Swabia.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaisheim">Kaisheim</a> Abbey was founded in 1133 by Count Henry of Lechsgemünd and settled by Cistercian monks. It gained imperial protection early, but its status was disputed for centuries with the dukes of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a> and the counts of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/county-of-oettingen">Oettingen</a>, until its immediacy was recognised in 1656.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The abbot ruled as a prelate of the Empire, sitting on the Swabian bench of prelates in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/imperial-diet">Imperial Diet</a>. The abbey held lands, villages and town houses around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lech-river">Lech</a>, collecting rents and tithes. It built a large baroque complex at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kaisheim">Kaisheim</a> and was known for its library and its imperial hall, the Kaisersaal.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In the secularisation of 1802 and 1803 the abbey was dissolved and its lands given to the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/electorate-of-bavaria">Electorate of Bavaria</a>. The buildings became a prison, which they still house today.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/imperial-abbey-of-kaisheim.jpg" alt="Imperial Abbey of Kaisheim" width="320" height="431"><figcaption>Image: Elise Bössl, Kaisheim, circa 1910. Reproduction of 1700 engraving. · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Engraving_of_Kaisersheim_(Kaisheim)_Abbey,_circa_1700..jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1656 CE – 1802 CE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> – <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>prince bishopric</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Kaisheim</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Catholicism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Kaisheim Abbey</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1729&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-kaisheim" aria-label="Imperial Abbey of Kaisheim on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4930 474 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Imperial Abbey of Kaisheim lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5250 730L5229 694M5250 730L5269 749"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5229 694h0M5269 749h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5250 730h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5250 730h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1729&amp;focus=imperial-abbey-of-kaisheim">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
